WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.909
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:12.160
Today we are going to see how we can use our
hands, but not our fingers to mingle with

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.780
objects in virtual worlds.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:19.800
The promise of virtual reality, VR is indeed
truly incredible.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:25.560
If one day it comes to fruition, doctors could
be trained to perform surgery in a virtual

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:31.990
environment, expose astronauts to virtual
zero-gravity simulations, work together with

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:35.140
telepresence applications, you name it.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:40.749
The dream is getting closer and closer, but
something is still missing!

00:00:40.749 --> 00:00:47.320
For instance, this previous work uses a learning-based
algorithm to teach a head-mounted camera to

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:51.859
tell the orientation of our hands at all times.

00:00:51.859 --> 00:00:56.480
One more paper down the line, this technique
appeared that can deal with examples with

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:04.960
challenging hand-hand interactions, deformations,
lots of self-contact and self-occlusion.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.720
This was absolutely amazing, because these
are not gloves.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:10.720
No-no.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.100
This is the reconstruction of the hand by
the algorithm.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:16.610
Absolutely amazing.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:23.660
However, it is slow, and mingling with other
objects is still, quite limited.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:26.030
So, what is missing?

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:27.390
What is left to be done here?

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:32.330
So, let’s have a look at today’s paper
and find out together.

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:37.620
This is its output…yes, mingling that looks
very natural.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:40.420
But, what is so interesting here?

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:44.210
The interesting part is that it has realistic
finger movements.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:50.700
Well, that means, that it just reads the data
from sensors on the fingers, right?

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:58.240
Now, hold on to your papers, and we’ll find
out once we look at the input…oh my!

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:00.090
Is this really true?

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:03.430
No sensors on the fingers anywhere!

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:06.170
What kind of black magic is this?

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:13.680
And with that, we can now make the most important
observation in the paper: it reads information

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.599
from only the wrist and the objects in the
hand.

00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:22.690
Look, the sensors are on these gloves, but
none are on the fingers.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:28.829
Once again: the sensors have no idea what
we are doing with our fingers, it only reads

00:02:28.829 --> 00:02:34.349
the movement of our wrist and the object,
and all the finger movement is synthesized

00:02:34.349 --> 00:02:36.150
by it automatically.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:37.569
Whoa!

00:02:37.569 --> 00:02:45.109
And, with this, we can not only have a virtual
version of our hand, but we can also manipulate

00:02:45.109 --> 00:02:49.010
virtual objects with very few sensor readings.

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:51.330
The rest is up to the AI to synthesize.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:58.450
This means that we can have a drink with a
friend online, use a virtual hammer to, depending

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:03.180
on our mood, fix or destroy virtual objects.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:08.629
This is very challenging because the finger
movements have to follow the geometry of the

00:03:08.629 --> 00:03:09.629
object.

00:03:09.629 --> 00:03:16.639
Look, here, the same hand is holding different
objects, and the AI knows how to synthesize

00:03:16.639 --> 00:03:20.060
the appropriate finger movements for both
of them.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:24.209
This is especially apparent when we change
the scale of the object.

00:03:24.209 --> 00:03:31.150
You see, the small one requires small and
precise finger movements to turn around, these

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:35.920
are motions that need to be completely re-synthesized
for the bigger objects.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.120
So cool.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:44.129
And now comes the key - so, does this only
work on objects that it has been trained on?

00:03:44.129 --> 00:03:46.310
No, not at all!

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:51.389
For instance, the method has not seen this
kind of teapot before, and still, it knows

00:03:51.389 --> 00:03:56.719
how to use its handle, and now to hold it
from the bottom too, even if both of these

00:03:56.719 --> 00:03:59.310
parts look different.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:03.999
Be careful though, who knows, maybe virtual
teapots can get hot too!

00:04:03.999 --> 00:04:09.430
What’s more, it also handles the independent
movement of the left and right hands.

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:12.769
Now, how fast is all this?

00:04:12.769 --> 00:04:15.599
Can we have coffee together in virtual reality?

00:04:15.599 --> 00:04:16.970
Yes, absolutely!

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:20.730
All this runs in close to real time!

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:27.400
There is a tiny bit of delay though, but,
a result like this is already amazing, and

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:32.250
this is typically the kind of thing that can
be fixed one more paper down the line.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:34.660
However, not even this technique is perfect.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:38.370
It still might miss small features on an object.

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:42.150
For instance, a very thin handle might confuse
it.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:49.289
Or, if it has an inaccurate reading of the
hand pose and distances, this might happen.

00:04:49.289 --> 00:05:45.819
But for now, having a virtual coffee together…yes
please, sign me up!

00:05:45.819 --> 00:05:49.670
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

